`REQUIRES_NEW` and `NESTED` both provide rollback isolation from the outer transaction, but they work through fundamentally different mechanisms with different failure characteristics.

**`REQUIRES_NEW` suspends the outer transaction** entirely and opens a new database connection (or borrows from the pool). The inner transaction commits or rolls back on its own connection. If the inner transaction commits before the outer fails, the inner commit is **permanent** — the database cannot undo it when the outer transaction rolls back on a different connection. This is exactly what audit logging needs: the log entry persists even if the business operation fails.

**`NESTED` uses a savepoint** within the same physical connection. When the inner method begins, Spring (via JDBC) issues `SAVEPOINT inner_tx`. If the inner method throws, Spring rolls back to the savepoint — undoing only the inner work. The outer transaction can catch the exception and continue. But if the outer transaction fails, the rollback undoes everything back to the start — including work done within the nested transaction, since it's all on the same connection.

```java
// REQUIRES_NEW: audit log survives business transaction failure
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void writeAuditLog(AuditEntry entry) {
    auditRepo.save(entry);  // commits on its own connection
}

// NESTED: optional enrichment — failure doesn't kill the outer operation
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NESTED)
public void enrichWithExternalData(Order order) {
    // if this fails, caller catches the exception and continues without enrichment
    ExternalData data = externalApiClient.fetch(order.getId());
    order.setExternalMetadata(data);
}
```

**The resource cost of `REQUIRES_NEW`** matters in high-throughput systems. Each `REQUIRES_NEW` call borrows a connection from the pool for the duration of the inner transaction, while the outer transaction still holds its connection. For a service that processes 100 requests/sec with 5 `REQUIRES_NEW` audit calls each, you're using 6× the connections you'd expect. Connection pool exhaustion under load is the failure mode. `NESTED` avoids this: one connection, one transaction, multiple savepoints.

